Concept: debugger

If you prefer a more graphical user interface, you can use the Gamma
Debugger, henceforth referred to as the def:Debugger.  The Debugger is
in essence a geometry viewing application, but it posseses some
features more common to a debugger, which make it particularly
convenient to use with a system like Gamma.  It also posseses a
similar interface.

Using the Debugger is more appropriate when developing a model, where
you would typically want to inspect the current results of your
program, make neccessary changes and repeat until the desired output
is achieved.  The simplest way to run the program contained in
f`cube.ext` through the Debugger, is by starting it as shown below.

```
gammadb --target cube --args cube.ext
```

This will start the debugger, which will print some introductory
messages, followed by the prompt s`#`.  The o`--target` and o`--args`
options provide the minimal configuration needed to run the program
and display the single output contained in it and will be fully
explained later.

You can now type the command c`run` at the prompt, which will invoke
Gamma as needed to compile the model and send it to the Debugger,
where it will be loaded and displayed in a window. The mouse rotates,
pans, or zooms the view when the cursor is dragged on the window with
the left, right and middle mouse buttons respectively.

If you were to decide that the cube isn't rounded quite enough, you
could simply edit f`cube.ext`, change the sphere radius from s`7/10`
to s`2/3` and repeat the run command^[You can do this by simply
pressing return, which is equivalent to entering the last command
again.].  The displayed model should change accordingly.

When you've finished working, enter the command c`exit`, or c`quit`,
or press k`Ctrl-D` to exit the Debugger.

Unalias: cube.ext

## A Program in Broad Strokes

Although very simple, the program shown in the previous section
illustrates the basic structure of most programs.  After importing
functions from one or more libraries, they are first used to create
def:primitive geometry, that is, geometry that isn't based on some
previous result, but is produced from scratch.  These are typically
simple geometric objects and are therefore rarely the intended output
of the program. Instead, they serve as basic building blocks which are
combined and further processed by functions that operate on them.

Here sphere and cube primitives are produced using the `sphere` and
`cuboid` functions imported from the `polyhedra` library and then
intersected using the `intersection` function imported from the
`operations` library.  By carefully chosing the sphere radius^[The
value s`7/10` is close to $\frac{\sqrt{2}}{2}$, so that the sphere
barely extends to the midpoints of the cube's faces.  Try changing
s`intersection` to s`union` and rerunning the program for an
illustration.]  most of the cube is inside the sphere; only its
corners extend out of it and are rounded off by the intersection.

Concept: outputs

Even though the program calls three functions, each of which adds an
def:operation that produces geometry, not all results are of immediate
interest.  The sphere and cube geometry are only needed to take their
intersection and produce the rounded cube, which is the intended
result of a program.  This result^[More correctly, the operation which
will produce the result.  In terms of the program, operations are
represented by the values returned by most, but not all, function
calls imported from Gamma's libraries.  Gamma keeps track of the
operations created by the program and subsequently evaluates them to
produce the desired outputs.] is therefore designated as an
def:output.  There can be more than one outputs and each can be saved
to disk or sent to the Debugger for inspection.

## A Few Examples

The following chapters describe all of Gamma's features in detail, but
before doing that, it might be helpful to give a few examples of
practical programs.  If you're new to Gamma, don't worry if you don't
understand them in detail.  Their purpose is to give you an idea of
how Gamma is used in practice and what you'll be able to do with it.
They will also serve as a more concrete basis for the introduction of
some of the fundamental concepts and techniques involved.

It is still recommended that you try to follow along by copying the
code and trying it out.  If you want to do so, you should create a
directory dedicated to the purpose, as you would for any program.
Besides keeping things organized, it will avoid flooding your home
directory (or the directory from which you would run Gamma) with a
multitude of files that Gamma often creates during compilation.

