## Program Parameters

After the obligatory library imports, the initial portion of the
program consists in the definition of def:parameters.  Functionally,
these are normal variables used much like configuration variables are
used in any program to represent parameters or quantities that might
need to change from one build to the next.

To avoid having to edit the source file to do so, their values can be
overriden on the command line, by passing the o`-D` option to Gamma.
They also highlight one of the most attractive features of this
approach to modeling.  While it might have been easier to design the
sprocket interactively in a graphical application, your effort to do
it in the form of a program is compensated by your ability to vary the
design arbitrarily.  Instead of *a* sprocket, you get *any* sprocket.

For instance, to produce a sprocket with 9 teeth you only need to run
Gamma with the o`-Dz=9` option, to redefine the variable `z`, which
represents the number of teeth, to 9.  You can also instruct the
Debugger to do this for you with the command s`define z 9`.  Try
entering it at the prompt and re-running the program.

## Working Interactively

The geometric calculations needed to produce the final model can be
quite intensive^[In the trade-off between computational speed and
correctness and robustness the emphasis is placed squarely on the
latter.  For instance, computations are carried out in exact
arithmetic on rational numbers, instead of the floating-point
arithmetic supported in hardware.] and can therefore take some time to
carry out.  When working on a design, making repeated changes to the
program and re-running, this can quickly become tiresome.  To avoid
testing your patience, Gamma has a couple of tricks to speed things
up.

Figure: sprocket
```print sprocket.scm
# set print-frames yes
# split horizontally 3 1
# split vertically 2
# focus 2
# target sprocket
# rotate 50 -50 -40
```
  The output of the sprocket example program, as seen in the Debugger.
  The upper left viewport shows the tooth tips, as produced by taking
  the intersectons of the tooth flank curves and the sprocket rim
  circle, while the lower left viewport shows the central plate.
  Finally, the right viewport shows the final part; the extruded union
  of the other two.

Firstly, only the necessary computations for the selected outputs are
carried out in a given run, as you can see for yourself in the
Debugger.  When you start it, the c`run` command inside the
initialization file will build all outputs.  Note the number of
required evaluation steps shown in the printed operations listing and
then place the mouse cursor in one of the viewports and press the k`r`
key.  Now only this output is rebuilt and the number of steps in the
listing should have gone down, along with the time needed to carry
them out.  As you will typically work on a small part of the design at
a time, this can be a big help.

Another feature that can help, is caching of the results of
intermediate operations.  When the cummulative evaluation time needed
to produce a result exceeds a certain threshold, it is written to disk
in the current directory, so that it can be reloaded during a future
build instead of computing it again from scratch.  This does not apply
only to the final result; every intermediate operation can be cached
in this way, so that if you only change a small part of your design,
much of the work needed to rebuild it can be avoided.

The current design can be evaluated relatively quickly, so you will
probably not see any files containing cached results if you list the
files in your directory.  This is in part due to the following trick.

The first defined parameter variable, `draft_p`, is a boolean which is
used to select the def:{curve tolerance}, a built-in parameter that
determines the tolerance used when approximating circles or circular
segments as polygons.  A lower tolerance will lead to more vertices in
the polygonal approximation and consequently more faces in their
extrusions.

The approach followed in the example, illustrates a sensible strategy
in selecting the curve tolerance.  Although `draft_p` is initially
false, the s`define draft` command inside the initialiazitaion file
f`.gammadbinit` instructs the Debugger to set it to true when
executing the program.  This selects a large tolerance, affording only
the detail necessary to determine whether the intended result has been
achieved.  It also keeps the geometric complexity and with it the
build time low.

When you want to produce the final model, you can re-run the program
with `draft_p` left false to select a smaller tolerance and hence
more detail.  Try entering the command s`undefine draft` at the
Debugger's prompt and re-running the program.  You will notice that
the shapes of the teeth are now smoother, but the build time was
substantially longer than before. If you now list the files in your
directory once more, you may notice that new files have been created
to cache some of the results of the more detailed build.  Their names
will be a long string of random-looking numbers and letters, ending in
the extension f`.zo`.

Of course the curve tolerance is just one parameter that influences
the geometric complexity of the produced model, but the same selection
strategy can be applied more broadly.^[For instance, you could use
`draft_p` to set the parameter determining the number of teeth, `z`,
to a lower number while working on the sprocket.]

## Designing a Sprocket

Being a toothed wheel, a sprocket is defined in terms of the disk from
which it is machined and the geometry of the parts that need to be cut
out from its rim to form the teeth.  These are called the "tooth gap
forms" in the standard and are based on two curves.

The roots of the gaps are formed by segments of the small circles
shown in ref:sprocket-diagram, subtending a specified angle, called
the "roller-seating angle" in the standard.  From there, arcs along
the larger circles are followed to form the flanks of the teeth, up to
the tips.

After calculating a number of derived quantities following the
formulas laid out in the standard^[For the tip diameter, a value
halfway between the smallest and largest diameters allowed in the
standard is used.] the program defines a utlity function that places
its argument relative to the roller seating curves.  Such placement
functions are very useful in practice, because they can exploit
symmetries in a design.  This one first rotates the given shape to
align it with tooth `n`, then translates it `r` units below the center
of the corresponding roller seating circle and rotates it by a
fraction `c` of the roller-seating angle relative to that center.  It
is used to make a polygon out of the seat-to-flank transition points
of each tooth by taking the convex hull of these points.  The result
is shown in dotted lines in ref:sprocket-diagram.

From it the roller seats are cut out by taking the difference with
circles placed using the same function.  This produces the central
part of the sprocket, shown in the bottom left frame of fig: sprocket.

The circles forming the flanks are tangent to the roller seating
curves, so the placement function can be used once more to transform
them into place^[The circle is translated vertically until its top is
tangent with the bottom of the corresponing roller seat curve and then
rotated by half the roller-seating angle.].  The sprocket teeth can
then be formed by taking appropriate intersections of these circles
and the sprocket's rim circle.  They are shown in the top left frame
of fig: sprocket.

